# Find Sources

Purpose: find real, checkable sources for a question and save only the ones the user chooses.
Everything lands in `~/Research/`, which is the system's memory between sessions.

## First run

If `~/Research/` does not exist, create it before searching:

```
~/Research/sources/   notes/   answers/   INDEX.md   AGENTS.md
```

`INDEX.md` starts with:

```markdown
# Research Index

One line per note: `- <note-file> | <year> | tier <A-D> | <key claims>`
```

`AGENTS.md` holds this text and nothing else:

```markdown
# Research Folder

This folder is a research library, not a code project.

- `sources/` holds PDFs and saved pages. Never edit or delete a source.
- `notes/` holds one note per source: citation, quotable claims, method, limits.
- `answers/` holds written syntheses.
- `INDEX.md` lists every note with its key claims. Read it first, always.
- Cite or do not claim. A claim without a quote and locator is not allowed.
- Say "not covered by your sources" rather than answering from memory.
```

Never touch anything else in the home directory, and never create a git repository here.

## Keys

Two keys, one file: `~/Research/.env`, loaded at startup by the Pi extension when `pi` is launched
from this folder. Never commit it; `chmod 600 ~/Research/.env`.

```bash
# OpenAlex — free. One call with an email; the key arrives by link and works after the click.
OPENALEX_API_KEY=<key>
# Semantic Scholar — free form at semanticscholar.org/product/api; approval is not instant.
S2_API_KEY=<key>
```

Missing key behaviour, stated honestly: OpenAlex without a key shares a **$0.10/day budget with
everyone on the same IP address**, which runs out in a handful of searches — treat the key as
required, not optional. Semantic Scholar without a key rate-limits hard (429), so results arrive
slowly and incompletely rather than not at all.

## Search

Run all three; they cover different literature. Wrap the user's words in one query each — do not
stack synonyms.

```bash
# OpenAlex — broad academic coverage; send the key or the shared budget dies
curl -sG "https://api.openalex.org/works" \
  --data-urlencode "search=<query>" \
  --data-urlencode "per_page=10" \
  --data-urlencode "api_key=${OPENALEX_API_KEY}" \
  --data-urlencode "select=id,title,publication_year,cited_by_count,doi,type,open_access,authorships"

# Semantic Scholar — citation counts and abstracts; needs S2_API_KEY
curl -s -H "x-api-key: ${S2_API_KEY}" \
  "https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/search?query=<urlencoded>&limit=10&fields=title,year,venue,citationCount,externalIds,url,abstract"
```

Plus one web search through the harness's web tool for practitioner and grey literature. If an API
returns 429, wait 3 seconds and retry once, then continue with the sources that did work and say which
one failed.

## Rank

Give every candidate a line of reasoning, not just a URL:

- **Relevance** — which part of the question it can answer, and which part it cannot.
- **Tier** — `A` peer-reviewed primary study, `B` peer-reviewed review or preprint, `C` official
  documentation or first-hand practitioner report, `D` commentary.
- **Independence** — mark candidates that report the same underlying study. Three write-ups of one
  trial are one source, and saying so is the whole point of the tier.

Prefer the direct source over an aggregator. A blog summarising a paper is a lead; the paper is
evidence.

## Save

Present the table and ask which sources to save. Do not download before the answer.

```bash
mkdir -p ~/Research/sources
curl --fail --location --connect-timeout 15 --max-time 60 \
  --output ~/Research/sources/<firstauthor>-<year>-<short-title>.pdf "<pdf-url>"
head -c 5 ~/Research/sources/<file>.pdf   # must print %PDF-
```

Check the file is non-empty and starts with `%PDF-`; an HTML error page saved as `.pdf` is a failed
download, so delete it and report the failure. Prefer open-access locations (arXiv, PMC, DOAJ,
`open_access.oa_url` from OpenAlex). When a source is paywalled, say so and leave a line for the user
to add it themselves.

Then append one line per saved source to `INDEX.md`:

```markdown
- <firstauthor>-<year>-<short-title> | <year> | tier B | not read yet
```

## Rules

- Never present a source as support for a claim without naming the claim.
- Never invent a citation, DOI, or arXiv ID. Verify an identifier against the source page before writing it.
- If nothing usable comes back, say that plainly. Remembered literature is not a search result.
- Do not summarise what the paper found. Read it with `/research-read` first.
